{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "chess-visualizer",
  "main": "worker/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-08-01",
  // The built app. Anything matching a file in here is served as-is; everything
  // else falls through to the Worker above, which is how /ws reaches it.
  "assets": {
    "directory": "./dist",
    "binding": "ASSETS",
    // The app is one page that reads its own address, so a path it does not
    // have a file for is still the app: an invite link points at /game?id=...,
    // which is nothing on disk and must not come back as a 404.
    "not_found_handling": "single-page-application"
  },
  /*
    What a deployed worker is told. A game says what it is doing — see
    `worker/log.ts` — and a busy deployment saying it for every game of every
    reader is a great deal of writing nobody asked for, so it is off here and
    on in `.dev.vars`, which is what a machine running this locally reads.
    Turn it on for a deployment when there is a question to answer:

      npx wrangler deploy --var GAME_LOG:on
  */
  "vars": { "GAME_LOG": "off" },
  /*
    How long idle games are kept goes by how many there are, and the numbers
    of that are variables too, each with a default in worker/keeping.ts:

      KEEP_N1 5000          never evicted at this many games or fewer
      KEEP_N2 50000         no new game at this many
      KEEP_T0_MS 2 days     the least a game is kept, at N2
      KEEP_T1_MS 30 days    what it gets beyond that at half-way
      KEEP_FINISHED_SHARE 0.5   a finished game's keep, as a share

    Set like GAME_LOG, with --var on a deploy. KEEPING_TEST, which opens the
    registry to questions from tests, is for `wrangler dev` only: see
    .dev.vars.

    ADMIN_TOKEN, what GET /admin/api/health asks for, is a secret rather than a
    variable, so that it is in no file and no command line's history:

      npx wrangler secret put ADMIN_TOKEN

    Without it the admin routes are not there at all. Asked with
    `Authorization: Bearer <token>`; see worker/index.ts.
  */

  // One object per game, addressed by the game's id; and one registry
  // counting them, which decides how long an idle game is kept — see
  // worker/keeping.ts.
  "durable_objects": {
    "bindings": [
      { "name": "GAME", "class_name": "Game" },
      { "name": "REGISTRY", "class_name": "Registry" }
    ]
  },
  // SQLite-backed, which is the storage the free plan offers. The registry
  // came later, so it is a migration of its own.
  "migrations": [
    { "tag": "v1", "new_sqlite_classes": ["Game"] },
    { "tag": "v2", "new_sqlite_classes": ["Registry"] }
  ],
  /*
    Keep what the Worker says, rather than only showing it to whoever happens
    to be watching. `wrangler tail` is a live stream and nothing more: a run
    that fails while nobody is attached leaves nothing behind at all, which is
    how a failure comes to be unexplainable. With this, the logs are held by
    Cloudflare and can be read in the dashboard afterwards.

    Sampling is left at everything. This is a small app with few requests, and
    the whole value here is that the one interesting request was kept.
  */
  "observability": { "enabled": true, "head_sampling_rate": 1 }
}
